task_type_fields.proposal:
Send an out-of-band notification (SASE notification inbox, which fans out to Telegram and mobile) when a bead first becomes stranded, so a human learns about it without having ACE open.

Epic sase-1no added one classifier for this: the sase-core `stranded_work_reduce` reducer behind `sase.bead.stranded.load_stranded_report(project)`. ACE shows its verdicts with amber ⚐ flags, a startup-quiet toast (`src/sase/ace/tui/actions/agents/_stranded_toasts.py`), and a one-keystroke resolve modal, and `sase doctor` prints a WARNING. All of these only reach a human who is already looking at ACE or running doctor.

The motivating incident (sase-1n6 E2, about 68 hours with no critical-path progress) went unnoticed because nobody was looking. A periodic host-side check could do this: an AXE chop or an existing scheduler lane that calls `load_stranded_report` for each enabled project. It would diff leaf sets against a small persisted baseline, the way the toast does in memory, and emit one coalesced notification per newly stranded leaf. Each notification would carry the cause sentence from `sase.bead.stranded_presentation` and both remedies: `%id(bead=<id>) #fork:<last turn>` and `sase bead close <id> --note "…"`.

Design points to settle:
- dedupe across restarts;
- respect the reducer's 120 s settle grace;
- never notify on `unknown` verdicts;
- which project set to scan.
